## Why This Question Matters
This question tests your ability to handle late data gracefully without breaking at-least-once delivery guarantees. It probes your understanding of per-topic state management, routing decisions under delayed data conditions, and offset tracking.

## Key Concepts
- Per-topic watermarks for independent time-window tracking
- Late data routing based on watermark thresholds
- Offset tracking and message deduplication for at-least-once semantics
- Canary testing to validate both in-order and late-data paths

## Code Example
```javascript
class WatermarkManager {
  constructor(maxLagMs = 60000) {
    this.maxLagMs = maxLagMs;
    this.topicWatermarks = new Map();
    this.offsetStore = new Map();
  }

  processMessage(topic, partition, offset, timestamp) {
    const key = `${topic}-${partition}`;
    const currentWatermark = this.topicWatermarks.get(key) || 0;

    if (timestamp > currentWatermark) {
      this.topicWatermarks.set(key, timestamp);
    }

    const watermark = this.topicWatermarks.get(key);
    const lag = watermark - timestamp;

    this.offsetStore.set(key, offset);

    if (lag > this.maxLagMs) {
      return { route: 'late_events', topic, partition, offset };
    }

    return { route: 'main', topic, partition, offset };
  }
}
```

This implementation maintains per-topic watermarks, routes late messages appropriately, tracks offsets, and supports at-least-once delivery through committed offset storage.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical error handling, DLQ patterns, offset management, and testability in a beginner KCNA context.

## Key Concepts
- JSON deserialization and field validation
- Dead-letter queue (DLQ) strategy
- Per-message offset handling and commit semantics
- Idempotent sinks and at-least-once delivery guarantees

## Code Example
```javascript
// Consumer API surface
interface ConsumerContext {
  message: {
    key: Buffer;
    value: Buffer;
    partition: number;
    offset: bigint;
  };
  emit(topic: string, key: Buffer, value: Buffer): Promise<void>;
  commit(): Promise<void>;
}

type MessageHandler = (ctx: ConsumerContext) => Promise<void>;

// Implementation
async function onMessage(ctx: ConsumerContext): Promise<void> {
  try {
    const m = JSON.parse(ctx.message.value.toString());

    if (!m.orderId || !m.customerId || m.total == null) {
      throw new Error('missing required fields: orderId, customerId, total');
    }

    const enriched = {
      ...m,
      processedAt: new Date().toISOString(),
      sourcePartition: ctx.message.partition,
      sourceOffset: ctx.message.offset.toString()
    };

    await ctx.emit('orders_enriched', ctx.message.key, JSON.stringify(enriched));
    await ctx.commit();
  } catch (err) {
    const dlqRecord = {
      originalKey: ctx.message.key?.toString() ?? null,
      partition: ctx.message.partition,
      offset: ctx.message.offset.toString(),
      error: err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err),
      timestamp: new Date().toISOString()
    };

    await ctx.emit('dead_letter', ctx.message.key, JSON.stringify(dlqRecord));
    await ctx.commit();
  }
}
```

